1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,371
Hello, and welcome.

2
00:00:01,371 --> 00:00:09,890
This is CareSlot, a clinic appointment scheduler, and the DevSecOps pipeline that takes it from a laptop all the way to a monitored Kubernetes cluster.

3
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:14,460
I'll start with the slides, and then do a live walkthrough of everything running.

4
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:19,594
Small clinics still book appointments on paper, or in shared spreadsheets.

5
00:00:19,594 --> 00:00:26,449
Two receptionists can book the same doctor at the same time, and nobody really knows who is sitting in the waiting room.

6
00:00:26,449 --> 00:00:27,592
CareSlot fixes that.

7
00:00:27,592 --> 00:00:31,077
Its main rule is simple: a doctor can never be double-booked.

8
00:00:31,976 --> 00:00:37,565
The application is a React front desk, a FastAPI backend, and a Postgres database.

9
00:00:37,565 --> 00:00:40,701
It manages doctors, patients and appointments.

10
00:00:40,701 --> 00:00:46,290
Every appointment follows a workflow, from scheduled, to checked in, to completed.

11
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:52,629
And if a booking overlaps one the doctor already has, the API refuses it with a 409 conflict.

12
00:00:53,543 --> 00:00:55,786
Here is the stack, layer by layer.

13
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:59,678
React, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy and Alembic for the application.

14
00:00:59,678 --> 00:01:01,525
Pytest and Ruff for quality.

15
00:01:01,525 --> 00:01:05,022
Multi-stage Docker images that run as non-root users.

16
00:01:05,022 --> 00:01:08,716
GitHub Actions and the GitHub container registry for CI.

17
00:01:08,716 --> 00:01:10,563
Trivy for security scanning.

18
00:01:10,563 --> 00:01:13,070
Terraform for the AWS network and EKS.

19
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:16,368
Helm, ingress engine X and Argo CD for Kubernetes.

20
00:01:16,368 --> 00:01:20,393
And the kube-prometheus stack with Grafana for observability.

21
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:22,837
This is the architecture.

22
00:01:22,837 --> 00:01:24,487
A developer pushes to main.

23
00:01:24,487 --> 00:01:31,820
GitHub Actions tests and scans the code, builds the images, and pushes them to the registry, tagged with the commit SHA.

24
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,142
CI then writes that SHA back into Git.

25
00:01:34,142 --> 00:01:37,136
Argo CD notices the change and syncs the cluster.

26
00:01:37,136 --> 00:01:41,231
Prometheus scrapes the backend, and Grafana shows what's happening.

27
00:01:42,143 --> 00:01:43,876
The pipeline has six stages.

28
00:01:43,876 --> 00:01:51,612
Four quality gates run in parallel: lint and tests, the frontend build, Terraform validate with helm lint, and a secret scan.

29
00:01:51,612 --> 00:01:54,582
The images are built once, and Trivy scans them.

30
00:01:54,582 --> 00:01:56,377
Only clean images are pushed.

31
00:01:56,377 --> 00:02:03,308
Then a throwaway kind cluster installs the Helm chart and books a real appointment, before anything is promoted.

32
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:06,293
And Trivy caught a real problem.

33
00:02:06,293 --> 00:02:17,167
The very first backend image failed the gate on seven high severity findings, including C V E 2025 62727, a denial of service bug in Starlette's Range header handling.

34
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:21,269
I upgraded the stack and stripped pip out of the runtime image.

35
00:02:21,269 --> 00:02:25,371
Both images now scan with zero fixable high or critical issues.

36
00:02:26,276 --> 00:02:38,535
For infrastructure, there are two Terraform modules: a VPC with two public and two private subnets and a NAT gateway, and an EKS cluster with a managed node group and KMS-encrypted secrets.

37
00:02:38,535 --> 00:02:46,967
I ran plan, apply and destroy against a local AWS emulator with dummy credentials, so the lifecycle is real, but it costs nothing.

38
00:02:47,876 --> 00:02:59,650
On Kubernetes, a Helm chart deploys two replicas of each service, cluster IP services, a Postgres stateful set, and an ingress that sends slash to the frontend and slash A P I to FastAPI.

39
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:05,316
Pods run as non-root with read-only file systems, and the namespace enforces Pod Security.

40
00:03:05,316 --> 00:03:07,457
Argo CD does all of the deploying.

41
00:03:08,357 --> 00:03:10,965
The backend has a horizontal pod autoscaler.

42
00:03:10,965 --> 00:03:19,855
Under sixty concurrent workers, the backend went from two pods to six, the configured maximum, and it scaled back down after the stabilisation window.

43
00:03:19,855 --> 00:03:22,522
You'll see this happen live in a few minutes.

44
00:03:23,424 --> 00:03:29,367
For observability, a service monitor gets Prometheus scraping the backend's metrics endpoint.

45
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:38,634
The Grafana dashboard shows traffic, latency, errors, CPU per pod, and business metrics, like bookings by specialty and rejected double-bookings.

46
00:03:39,535 --> 00:03:40,839
Four things I learned.

47
00:03:40,839 --> 00:03:43,802
Scanners find real problems, not theoretical ones.

48
00:03:43,802 --> 00:03:53,641
Start-up order is a deployment concern: two replicas racing the database migration crash-looped until I added an advisory lock and a wait-for-database init container.

49
00:03:53,641 --> 00:03:56,427
GitOps makes the cluster boring, in a good way.

50
00:03:56,427 --> 00:04:01,109
And the starter code had real bugs, like an ingress pointing at the wrong port.

51
00:04:01,109 --> 00:04:02,710
Troubleshooting is the job.

52
00:04:03,610 --> 00:04:04,694
That's the overview.

53
00:04:04,694 --> 00:04:06,267
Now let's see it all running.

54
00:04:07,177 --> 00:04:09,157
First, the codebase, on GitHub.

55
00:04:09,157 --> 00:04:14,012
This is my fork of the starter repository, with the full history of commits.

56
00:04:14,012 --> 00:04:22,381
The repo has the backend, the frontend, the Helm chart, Terraform, Argo CD, monitoring and the CI workflow, each in its own folder.

57
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:35,477
In the appointments router, ALLOWED TRANSITIONS defines the status workflow, and ensure slot free is the double-booking rule: it looks for any overlapping appointment for the same doctor, and raises a 409.

58
00:04:35,477 --> 00:04:41,546
The test fixtures swap the real database for in-memory S Q Lite, so tests never touch Postgres.

59
00:04:41,546 --> 00:04:44,932
The Dockerfile is multi-stage and runs as user 10001.

60
00:04:44,932 --> 00:04:56,814
Then the Helm templates, the VPC and EKS Terraform modules, the Argo CD application, which points at the Helm chart on the main branch with automated sync, prune and self-heal turned on.

61
00:04:56,814 --> 00:05:01,350
And finally the CI workflow, which runs everything you're about to see.

62
00:05:16,987 --> 00:05:19,649
Here is the app, served through the ingress.

63
00:05:19,649 --> 00:05:24,127
I try to book Ananya with an orthopedist at a time the doctor already has.

64
00:05:24,127 --> 00:05:27,697
The API rejects it with a conflict, and the form shows why.

65
00:05:27,697 --> 00:05:30,602
I pick a free time and the booking goes through.

66
00:05:30,602 --> 00:05:33,688
Then I check the patient in and complete the visit.

67
00:05:33,688 --> 00:05:41,918
The patients tab has search, the doctors tab shows who is on duty, and FastAPI generates interactive API docs for all sixteen endpoints.

68
00:05:41,918 --> 00:05:43,431
Notice the sidebar label.

69
00:05:43,431 --> 00:05:45,125
We'll change it in a moment.

70
00:05:46,026 --> 00:05:47,592
Now CI and CD, end to end.

71
00:05:47,592 --> 00:05:50,965
I make a visible change: a release label in the sidebar.

72
00:05:50,965 --> 00:05:56,748
First the recent history, then a one-line change to the sidebar label, and the diff to prove it.

73
00:05:56,748 --> 00:05:58,495
I commit it and push to main.

74
00:05:58,495 --> 00:06:00,965
That push is the only thing I do by hand.

75
00:06:00,965 --> 00:06:03,073
Everything after this is automated.

76
00:06:05,559 --> 00:06:08,932
The push triggers the CareSlot pipeline in GitHub Actions.

77
00:06:08,932 --> 00:06:12,131
The four quality gates are already running in parallel.

78
00:06:12,131 --> 00:06:25,044
The backend job lints with Ruff and runs pytest, the frontend job runs the production build, the infrastructure job runs terraform validate and helm lint, and the security job scans the whole repository for leaked secrets.

79
00:06:25,044 --> 00:06:29,115
The image jobs for the backend and frontend wait for all four to pass.

80
00:06:29,115 --> 00:06:31,907
If any gate fails, nothing gets built or pushed.

81
00:06:31,907 --> 00:06:35,455
After the images, the deploy and GitOps jobs run in sequence.

82
00:06:36,359 --> 00:06:49,655
From the terminal, every job finished green: security, frontend, Terraform and Helm validation, backend tests, both image builds with their Trivy gates, the deploy smoke test on kind, and the GitOps promotion.

83
00:06:49,655 --> 00:06:51,500
About three minutes in total.

84
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,966
In the browser, the run graph is all green.

85
00:06:54,966 --> 00:07:00,935
The registry has the new backend image, tagged with the full commit SHA, and there is no latest tag.

86
00:07:00,935 --> 00:07:08,397
In the commit history, the CI bot has added a GitOps commit, and in the values file, the image tags now point at the new SHA.

87
00:07:08,397 --> 00:07:10,545
That commit is what Argo CD deploys.

88
00:07:10,545 --> 00:07:15,559
Notice it's marked skip C I, so the bot's commit doesn't trigger the pipeline again.

89
00:07:15,559 --> 00:07:23,976
The whole path, from my push to a promotable release, took a little over three minutes, with no manual steps and no credentials on my laptop.

90
00:07:32,033 --> 00:07:33,883
Locally, the same checks pass.

91
00:07:33,883 --> 00:07:38,507
Ruff is clean, and twenty-one tests pass with ninety-four percent coverage.

92
00:07:38,507 --> 00:07:46,399
Here is Trivy against the original backend image: exit code one, seven high findings, including the Starlette denial of service.

93
00:07:46,399 --> 00:07:50,468
And against the fixed image: exit code zero, nothing fixable left.

94
00:07:50,468 --> 00:07:56,325
In CI, this exact check is the gate that decides whether an image is allowed into the registry.

95
00:07:58,500 --> 00:07:59,586
Next, Terraform.

96
00:07:59,586 --> 00:08:03,320
The root module wires together the VPC and EKS modules.

97
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:15,470
terraform plan shows twenty-eight resources to add: the VPC, four subnets, the internet and NAT gateways, route tables, IAM roles, the KMS key, the EKS cluster and its node group.

98
00:08:15,470 --> 00:08:17,575
Apply creates all twenty-eight.

99
00:08:17,575 --> 00:08:22,462
Through the AWS CLI, the EKS cluster reports active, on Kubernetes 1.33.

100
00:08:22,462 --> 00:08:25,449
Then destroy removes all twenty-eight again.

101
00:08:25,449 --> 00:08:30,201
This runs against moto, a local AWS emulator, using dummy credentials.

102
00:08:30,201 --> 00:08:34,613
Against a real account, it's exactly the same terraform commands.

103
00:08:34,613 --> 00:08:43,777
The provider is configured so that one environment variable points it at the emulator, and nothing in the modules themselves is mocked.

104
00:08:43,777 --> 00:08:53,620
The tfvars example file documents the region, cluster version and node sizes, and the defaults are sized for a small clinic: two t3 medium nodes.

105
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:07,889
Here the AWS CLI reads back the VPC, its subnets across two availability zones, the EKS cluster and the node group, standing in for the AWS console.

106
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:09,558
Now Argo CD.

107
00:09:09,558 --> 00:09:12,527
The CareSlot application is synced and healthy.

108
00:09:12,527 --> 00:09:20,169
Its latest sync is the GitOps commit that CI made a few minutes ago, deployed automatically, with nobody running kubectl.

109
00:09:20,169 --> 00:09:22,695
The history shows each previous release.

110
00:09:22,695 --> 00:09:27,180
To show self-healing, I scale the frontend down to one replica by hand.

111
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:33,622
Argo CD detects the drift, the app goes to progressing, and it restores two replicas, back to healthy.

112
00:09:33,622 --> 00:09:46,191
The tree view shows every resource Argo CD manages: the deployments, replica sets and pods, the services, the ingress, the stateful set, the autoscaler, the service monitor and the Grafana dashboard.

113
00:09:47,371 --> 00:09:49,314
The same thing from the terminal.

114
00:09:49,314 --> 00:09:51,551
The application is synced and healthy.

115
00:09:51,551 --> 00:09:53,199
I scale the frontend to one.

116
00:09:53,199 --> 00:09:56,084
For a few seconds, only one of two pods is ready.

117
00:09:56,084 --> 00:10:02,148
Within about ten seconds, it's back to two out of two, and the application is still synced and healthy.

118
00:10:02,148 --> 00:10:05,739
Git is the source of truth, and manual drift doesn't survive.

119
00:10:10,705 --> 00:10:12,722
Here is the cluster with kubectl.

120
00:10:12,722 --> 00:10:16,513
All pods are running: two backend, two frontend, and Postgres.

121
00:10:16,513 --> 00:10:20,976
Then the services, the ingress on careslot dot local, and the autoscaler.

122
00:10:20,976 --> 00:10:24,705
Helm list shows the staging release and the monitoring stack.

123
00:10:24,705 --> 00:10:28,863
Both deployments run the image tagged with the commit I just pushed.

124
00:10:28,863 --> 00:10:31,308
And the API answers through the ingress.

125
00:10:34,705 --> 00:10:36,654
Now the autoscaler under load.

126
00:10:36,654 --> 00:10:40,489
I start a load test with sixty workers and watch the H P A.

127
00:10:40,489 --> 00:10:47,313
CPU jumps well past the sixty percent target, and replicas climb from two, to three, to six, the maximum.

128
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:48,678
This clip is sped up.

129
00:10:49,578 --> 00:10:57,408
In Prometheus, the targets page shows the CareSlot backend service monitor, with every backend pod up, six out of six while scaled out.

130
00:10:57,408 --> 00:11:02,513
A query for requests per second per pod shows the traffic spreading across the new pods.

131
00:11:02,513 --> 00:11:09,068
And the custom business metric counts appointments booked, labelled by specialty, coming from the app's own code.

132
00:11:11,483 --> 00:11:14,184
Finally, Grafana, live during the load test.

133
00:11:14,184 --> 00:11:16,701
Requests per second climb past a hundred.

134
00:11:16,701 --> 00:11:25,359
Latency percentiles, the five hundred error ratio stays at zero, and the backend pod count goes from three to six as the autoscaler responds.

135
00:11:25,359 --> 00:11:27,876
CPU per pod spreads out as new pods join.

136
00:11:27,876 --> 00:11:33,340
Further down are bookings by specialty, and status changes with rejected double-bookings.

137
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:42,059
The dashboard is shipped as a config map in the Helm chart, so Grafana loads it automatically, and it's versioned in Git like everything else.

138
00:11:42,059 --> 00:11:43,655
This clip is also sped up.

139
00:11:50,783 --> 00:11:51,573
So, to recap.

140
00:11:51,573 --> 00:11:57,286
Every change goes through tests and a security gate, and every image is traceable to a commit.

141
00:11:57,286 --> 00:12:04,154
Git is the only way into the cluster, Argo CD keeps it that way, and Prometheus and Grafana show what it's doing.

142
00:12:04,154 --> 00:12:05,552
Thank you for watching.

